  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.2. Increased capacity of member States to strengthen skills and lifelong learning policies, governance models and financing system</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. The National Council of Vocational Education and Training (NCVET) introduced a new credit transfer system for enhancing mobility of students and workers in the formal education system as a key mechanism to support lifelong learning.
2. The new NCVET framework to standardise quality assurance arrangements was developed by the Ministry of Skill to improve governance in the system. The new national quality assurance framework includes tools and resources that will allow awarding bodies, assessment agencies and training organisations to implement the new quality framework for training.
3. The NCVET has developed a proposal to establish a research unit to promote a research culture in the skills system and to establish a monitoring and evaluation capacity within NCVET. Working with the national regulatory body (NCVET), the ILO: 1) developed a draft national quality assurance framework and tools to allow awarding bodies, assessment agencies and training organisations implement the new quality framework for training; 2) provided technical inputs as a member of Core Committee for development of Credit Framework; and 3) developed the outline, terms of reference and monitoring framework for establishment of the new research unit.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">5.2.1. Number of member States with inclusive skills and lifelong learning strategies</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship undertook a skill needs study in 7 sub-sectors of manufacturing sector with high share of Gross Value Addition to identify segments which are competitive and demonstrate potential for growth and employment; and the granular details of the skill-sets which are/shall be required in these segments. The study findings fed into designing of the Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana 3.0 : 2020-21 (a short-term skilling flagship programme of Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship).  The scheme was implemented on pilot basis during the year 2020- 21 known as Special Projects PMKVY 3.0. The 3.0 scheme shall initiate the creation of implementation framework for the second phase (2021-2026) of the scheme to scale-up formal acquisition of skills, particularly in manufacturing sector. ILO provided technical support in design of survey instruments, sampling and gathering data for the Ministry study, besides review of the report.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">5.1.1. Number of member States that have applied ILO approaches to measure skills mismatches and/or anticipate future skill needs at national and/or sectoral level</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
 </iati-activity>
